    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.animate\n\n### Description\n\nThanks for this great plugin! I love `mini.animate` along with some of your other plugins, but thought it'd be great to be able to disable scroll animation after search (`/`).\r\n\r\nIf the search result is outside my current window, Nvim already moves my window to show the result even before I type `<CR>`. When I type `<CR>`, without `mini.animate` turned on, both the cursor and window just stay there. But with `mini.animate`'s scrolling animation turned on, the window screen moves back up to my original cursor position and comes back down to the search result. That's quite dizzying.\r\n\r\nSimilar to [LazyVim's config](https://github.com/LazyVim/LazyVim/blob/main/lua/lazyvim/plugins/extras/ui/mini-animate.lua), which disables animation on mouse scrolling, I set up the following keymap and it seems to work. But I'm not sure if this is the best way to do this.\r\n```lua\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"c\", \"<CR>\", function()\r\n  disable_animation = true\r\n  return \"<CR>\"\r\nend, { expr = true })\r\n```\r\n\r\nI'd totally understand if this isn't something you'd like to add officially to the plugin to keep it minimal. But I thought this is highly aligned with the goal of this plugin which is to help our brains cope with context switching better.",
